[A repost - Carl]

[Summary: Penn State U.'s 3-year-old ban of alt.sex violates academic
and intellectual freedom principles.]

From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,alt.homosexual
Subject:  Re: Should censor wannabe be censored now?
Date: 27 Mar 1994 17:51:04 -0500
Message-ID: <2n52ko$7on@eff.org>

>There is a mild form of censorship that universities place on accounts
>that they authorize. But most of their harsh action is against people
>posting messages that if spoken to their recipients would qualify as
>hateful speech, if not verbal assault and threats. Such things, I thought,
>are not protected under the Constitution anyway.

Hateful speech is generally protected by the Constitution because
government can't be trust to define "hateful", but we can be trusted
to counter hateful speech with the truth. [see refs]

>There also is the mild form of censorship that occurs when universities
>do not allow every newsgroup to be accessed (or even displayed). Penn
>State allows no access to alt.binaries.pictures.erotica or alt.sex...

If they banned alt.sex because it offends some, this is not a mild
form of censorship; this is a strong form of censorship. It may or may
not be illegal (see _Island Tree_), but it is a violation of academic
and intellectual freedom principles.

>I guess their justification is that they allow minors to have access, and
>therefore would be under possible criminal charges for handing a minor
>access to such material.

This is not what they said when they banned the newsgroups in 1991.
(They said it was for space, which doesn't wash because there are many
less valuable groups that take more room that alt.sex.). The
harmful-to-minors argument doesn't wash either. Most such laws exempt
universities and, moreover, even if the law did apply, the newsgroups
should only have been removed based on competent legal advice and an
advisorial hearing.

The American Library Association's statement on "Challenged Materials" says:
       
     Freedom of expression is protected by the Constitution of the United
     States, but constitutionally protected expression is often separated
     from unprotected expression only by a dim and uncertain line. The
     Constitution requires a procedure designed to focus searchingly on
     challenged expression before it can be suppressed. An adversary
     hearing is a part of this procedure.

- Carl

